  "query_preview": "/Through Wednesday/...\nIssued at 1231 PM MDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nA weak front is currently moving south through our forecast area\nas of 12:45pm. Behind the front, winds have turned more northerly,\nwith gusts around 15 to 25mph. Overall the impact of the front\nwill be limited, with the exception of the brief wind shift\ntoday. Light winds and mostly clear skies could allow for another\nmorning of low clouds and fog for the eastern plains. Weak \ndownsloping and lower moisture will likely prevent fog from \ndeveloping in the urban corridor, but we could see some patchy fog\nwith visiblities below 1 mile further east/northeast. Any low \nclouds and fog that developed overnight will dissipate by the mid\nto late morning. Moisture will increase at upper levels (300mb) \non Thursday, leading to an increase in high cloud cover for the \nafternoon. High temperatures will be a few degrees cooler \ntomorrow for much of the plains, compared to today.\n\nRemnants from Tropical Storm Vance are forecast to be absorbed \ninto the southwest flow aloft, moving through West Texas Thursday \nand Friday towards eastern CO and Kansas for late Friday. Right \nnow, models have the bulk of the moisture moving to our east. But,\na weak shortwave could still produce enough lift for some light \nrain showers across the far eastern plains late Friday. On \nSaturday, another shortwave is forecast to move over the area. But\nthis activity should primarily be confined to the mountains, \nwhere moisture will be more prevalent. \n\nThere is still a lot of uncertainty with the system late Sunday \ninto early next week. Here are a few things we do know with this \nsystem: a strong upper trough/low will move near or over the area\nsometime late Sunday into early Tuesday, a strong cold front will\nmove through at some point late Sunday into late Monday, and this\nsystem will bring strong lift and increased precipitation chances\nfor at least portions of the area. The things that are still very\nuncertain this far out and will have significant influence on the\npotential impacts in our area: the track of the upper level low \nand development of the surface low (aka the exact direction of the\nwinds/potential for upslope and area of highest forcing), the \nsurface temperatures behind the front which will have impacts on \nprecip type, how the subtropical jet and trough will interact with\nthe deep trough moving out of the Northwest, and if moisture will\nbe able to wrap around the low as it moves east of the area. \n\nModels are showing very strong winds behind the front, with speeds \naround 40kts to 70kts at 700mb on the back side of the low. If \nthe low ends up tracking in a way where there's a more easterly \ncomponent to these strong winds, precipitation chances and \namounts in the foothills and adjacent plains would look much more \npromising. Right now, the best chance for precipitation and snow \nwill be in the higher terrain with snow levels starting the event \naround 10kft to 11kft and gradually dropping closer to 6,500 to \n7,000ft by Tuesday morning. Snow chances for areas below 7,000 ft \nremain low (<15%) at this time. However, this is something we'll \nneed to keep a close eye on as we get closer to the event. \nFinally, with the strong winds and much colder conditions behind \nthe front, there is potential for wind chills in the upper teens \nacross portions of the plains Tuesday morning.",
